<%# The AIsuru side of the setup, deliberately kept OUTSIDE the manual guide.
    The two routes above (one-click starter and manual guide) are alternatives:
    you follow one or the other, but both end in the same place, registering the
    MCP server in AIsuru and loading the agent. So these steps live on their own.
    Locals:
      demo  - "demo1" | "demo2" | "demo3" | "demo6"
      color - CSS background for the header/step badges %>

<div class="row mb-4" id="aisuru-setup">
  <div class="col-lg-10 mx-auto">
    <div class="card shadow-sm border-primary">
      <div class="card-header text-white d-flex align-items-center" style="background: <%= color %>;">
        <i class="bi bi-robot me-2"></i>
        <h5 class="mb-0">Configure AIsuru: required in both cases</h5>
      </div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="alert alert-light border mb-4">
          <i class="bi bi-signpost-split me-1"></i>
          Above you pick <strong>one</strong> route: the
          <a href="#quick-start">one-click starter</a> <em>or</em> the
          <a href="#manual-setup">manual setup guide</a>. Both hand you the same values.
          <strong>What follows happens in AIsuru and is always required</strong>, whichever route you took.
        </div>

        <%# Step 1: register the MCP server in AIsuru %>
        <div class="d-flex mb-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 me-3">
            <span class="badge rounded-circle fs-5 d-flex align-items-center justify-content-center text-white" style="width: 40px; height: 40px; background: <%= color %>;">1</span>
          </div>
          <div class="flex-grow-1">
            <% case demo %>
            <% when "demo1" %>
              <h6 class="fw-bold">Add the built-in MongoDB MCP server</h6>
              <p class="text-muted mb-2">
                In AIsuru open your agent, go to <strong>MCP</strong> &rarr;
                <strong>MCP Servers</strong> and pick the ready-made <strong>MongoDB</strong> server
                from the list (no custom MCP needed here, AIsuru talks to the database directly).
                Fill in the connection details:
              </p>
              <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-bordered table-sm mb-2">
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light" style="width: 180px;">Connection String</td>
                      <td><code>mongodb://admin:adminpassword@<span class="text-danger">&lt;NGROK_HOST&gt;</span>:<span class="text-danger">&lt;NGROK_PORT&gt;</span>/mcp_demo?authSource=admin</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light">Database Name</td>
                      <td><code>mcp_demo</code></td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
              <small class="text-muted">
                Replace <code>&lt;NGROK_HOST&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;NGROK_PORT&gt;</code> with the TCP
                address of your tunnel. The one-click starter gives you the whole string already
                assembled. Example:
                <code>mongodb://admin:adminpassword@8.tcp.ngrok.io:12811/mcp_demo?authSource=admin</code>
              </small>

            <% when "demo2" %>
              <h6 class="fw-bold">Add the built-in MySQL MCP server</h6>
              <p class="text-muted mb-2">
                In AIsuru open your agent, go to <strong>Integrations</strong> &rarr;
                <strong>MCP Servers</strong> and pick the ready-made <strong>MySQL</strong> server
                from the list. It inspects the schema and runs the queries by connecting straight to
                the database, so no MCP server of your own is involved. Fill in the parameters:
              </p>
              <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-bordered table-sm mb-2">
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light" style="width: 180px;"><code>MYSQL_HOST</code></td>
                      <td><span class="text-danger">&lt;NGROK_HOST&gt;</span>: host of the TCP tunnel</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light"><code>MYSQL_PORT</code></td>
                      <td><span class="text-danger">&lt;NGROK_PORT&gt;</span>: port of the TCP tunnel</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light"><code>MYSQL_USER</code></td>
                      <td><code>mcpuser</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light"><code>MYSQL_PASS</code></td>
                      <td><code>mcppassword</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light"><code>MYSQL_DB</code></td>
                      <td><code>mcp_demo_mysql</code></td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
              <p class="small mb-2">
                <strong>⚠️ Leave <code>ALLOW_INSERT_OPERATION</code>, <code>ALLOW_UPDATE_OPERATION</code>,
                <code>ALLOW_DELETE_OPERATION</code> and <code>ALLOW_DDL_OPERATION</code> off</strong>
                until you actually want the agent to write: with them disabled it can only read.
              </p>
              <small class="text-muted">
                The tunnel address (e.g. <code>tcp://8.tcp.eu.ngrok.io:12345</code>) is the one from the
                one-click starter, or from <a href="http://localhost:4044" target="_blank" class="text-decoration-none">http://localhost:4044</a>
                if you started it by hand. Remember the tunnel is public while it is up, so stop it when you are done.
              </small>

            <% when "demo3" %>
              <h6 class="fw-bold">Add the MCP server as a <em>custom</em> MCP</h6>
              <p class="text-muted mb-2">
                This server is yours, not one of AIsuru's built-in connectors, so it does <strong>not</strong>
                appear in the list of ready-made servers. In AIsuru open your agent, go to
                <strong>Integrations</strong> &rarr; <strong>MCP Servers</strong> and use the
                <strong>&ldquo;Aggiungi MCP Personalizzato&rdquo;</strong>
                (<em>Add Custom MCP</em>) section, not the catalog above it.
                Paste the URL there:
              </p>
              <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-bordered table-sm mb-2">
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light" style="width: 150px;">MCP Server URL</td>
                      <td><code>https://<span class="text-danger">&lt;YOUR_NGROK_URL&gt;</span>/mcp</code></td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
              <small class="text-muted">
                Paste the string exactly as it is, <code>/mcp</code> endpoint included. That is the
                single value the one-click starter gives you. Example:
                <code>https://abc123.ngrok-free.app/mcp</code>.
                Save, and AIsuru will list the four tools
                (<code>list_files</code>, <code>read_file</code>, <code>write_file</code>, <code>create_file</code>).
              </small>

            <% when "demo6" %>
              <h6 class="fw-bold">Add the OAuth / API connector</h6>
              <p class="text-muted mb-2">
                You do not write or deploy an MCP server here: AIsuru builds one for you from
                the OpenAPI spec. In AIsuru open your agent, go to <strong>Integrations</strong>
                and add the <strong>OAuth / API</strong> connector. Once saved it shows up
                among your agent's <strong>MCP servers</strong>, with one tool per imported
                endpoint. Fill in these parameters, which the one-click starter above prints
                them already assembled:
              </p>
              <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-bordered table-sm mb-2">
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light" style="width: 200px;"><code>oauth_auth_type</code></td>
                      <td><code>oauth2_client_credentials</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light"><code>oauth_token_url</code></td>
                      <td><code>https://<span class="text-danger">&lt;YOUR_NGROK_URL&gt;</span>/token</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light"><code>oauth_client_id</code></td>
                      <td><code>aisuru-demo</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light"><code>oauth_client_secret</code></td>
                      <td><code>demo-secret-sys06</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light"><code>oauth_scope</code></td>
                      <td><code>commesse:read</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td class="fw-bold bg-light"><code>oauth_openapi_url</code></td>
                      <td><code>https://<span class="text-danger">&lt;YOUR_NGROK_URL&gt;</span>/openapi.json</code></td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
              <small class="text-muted">
                Leave <code>oauth_endpoints_json</code> empty: the endpoints are imported from
                the OpenAPI spec. Save, and the agent gains one tool per endpoint
                (<code>/commesse</code>, <code>/commesse/{id}</code>, <code>/clienti</code>,
                <code>/kpi/riepilogo</code>): four read-only tools.
                Only <code>https://</code> URLs are accepted.
              </small>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        </div>

        <%# Step 2: load the agent in this page %>
        <div class="d-flex mb-0">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 me-3">
            <span class="badge rounded-circle fs-5 d-flex align-items-center justify-content-center text-white" style="width: 40px; height: 40px; background: <%= color %>;">2</span>
          </div>
          <div class="flex-grow-1">
            <h6 class="fw-bold">Load the Agent Below &amp; Start Chatting</h6>
            <p class="text-muted mb-0">
              Enter your agent's credentials in the form below and click <strong>"Load Agent"</strong>.
              <% case demo %>
              <% when "demo1" %>
                The agent will be able to query and modify the MongoDB database via MCP.
                Use the <strong>DB Explorer</strong> panel on the right to see changes in real-time.
              <% when "demo2" %>
                The agent will be able to query and modify the MySQL database via MCP.
                Use the <strong>DB Explorer</strong> panel on the right to see changes in real-time.
              <% when "demo3" %>
                The agent will be able to manage files in the workspace via MCP.
                Use the <strong>Workspace Explorer</strong> panel on the right to see changes in real-time.
              <% when "demo6" %>
                The agent will be able to query and update the ERP through its REST API.
                Use the <strong>Live ERP data</strong> panel on the right to see changes in real-time.
              <% end %>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
